      "content": "The \"work-first\" arguments relying on tax incentives are likely a delaying tactic rather than a viable long-term strategy because automation evolves faster than tax codes can be implemented or adjusted. Companies often resist wage adjustments or subsidies to protect their profit margins, meaning these measures frequently fail to honor agreed-upon labor standards. Since automation can now perform tasks previously requiring human judgment without equally mandated labor costs, relying on corporate goodwill creates a fundamentally unsustainable economic model. Ultimately, waiting for companies to voluntarily retain human labor ignores the decisive velocity of technological displacement.",
      "source": "target",
